Experiment 7: Selection Sort on Random, Reverse and Sorted Arrays
Aim

To sort random, reverse-ordered and already-sorted arrays using Selection Sort, and to compare the number of comparisons and swaps in each case.

Algorithm
Start with i = 0.
Assume the element at position i is the smallest.
Compare it with all the elements to its right and find the position of the smallest one.
Swap the smallest element with the element at position i.
Increase i by 1 and repeat steps 2 to 4 until i = n-2.
Run the same steps on the random, reverse and sorted arrays and compare the counts.
Pseudo code
SelectionSort(A, n)
  for i = 0 to n-2
      min = i
      for j = i+1 to n-1
          comparisons++
          if A[j] < A[min]
              min = j
      if min != i
          swap A[i], A[min]
          swaps++
Source code (C)
c
#include <stdio.h>

void selectionSort(int a[], int n) {
    int i, j, min, temp;
    int comp = 0, swaps = 0;

    for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        min = i;
        for (j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            comp++;
            if (a[j] < a[min])
                min = j;
        }
        if (min != i) {
            temp = a[i];
            a[i] = a[min];
            a[min] = temp;
            swaps++;
        }
    }

    printf("Sorted   : ");
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
    printf("\nComparisons = %d, Swaps = %d\n\n", comp, swaps);
}

int main() {
    int random[8]  = {29, 10, 14, 37, 13, 5, 42, 20};
    int reverse[8] = {80, 70, 60, 50, 40, 30, 20, 10};
    int sorted[8]  = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80};

    printf("Random Array\n");
    selectionSort(random, 8);

    printf("Reverse Array\n");
    selectionSort(reverse, 8);

    printf("Sorted Array\n");
    selectionSort(sorted, 8);

    return 0;
}
Output
Random Array
Sorted   : 5 10 13 14 20 29 37 42 
Comparisons = 28, Swaps = 5

Reverse Array
Sorted   : 10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80 
Comparisons = 28, Swaps = 4

Sorted Array
Sorted   : 10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80 
Comparisons = 28, Swaps = 0
Result

Selection Sort sorted all three arrays correctly.

The number of comparisons is always n(n-1)/2 (28 for n = 8), whatever the order of the input. Only the number of swaps changes.
Time complexity: O(n²) in the best, average and worst cases.
Space complexity: O(1).
